course: dictionary
lesson: 4201
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Context, the words around the word, tells you which meaning to use. «The walkers stood at the edge of the gorge and looked down at the river.» Here the clues are the edge, the river and looking down, so a gorge is a deep, narrow valley with steep rocky sides.
explain: «Bees gorge on honey when they smell smoke.» Gorge is now a verb, a doing word, meaning to eat a lot, greedily, until very full. Check by swapping in a simpler word: bees eat on honey when they smell smoke. The meaning changes, so you need the clue of too much food.
explain: Old books use gorge for your throat. «It makes my gorge rise» means it makes me feel sick. This meaning is rare now, so if you meet it, look for words about feeling sick nearby.
fact: Gorge can be a noun (a gorge, gorges) or a verb (gorge, gorges, gorged, gorging).
fact: A gorge is a deep, narrow valley with steep rocky sides, usually with a river at the bottom.
fact: Gorges are often made by rivers cutting through rock over a very long time, like Cheddar Gorge in England.
fact: «Gorge yourself on cake» means to eat so much that you feel very full.
fact: «The Inca built roads with rope bridges over gorges.»
exercise: «The walkers stood at the edge of the gorge and looked down at the river.» What is a gorge here? | A tall tree with thick roots | A kind of bridge over a road | *A deep, narrow valley with steep rocky sides | A small pond in a garden
exercise: «The hungry puppy will gorge on its dinner.» What does gorge mean here? | *To eat a lot, greedily, until very full | To sleep for a long time | To run around the garden
exercise: «The Inca built roads with rope bridges over gorges.» Find and copy the word that names what the bridges crossed. | rope | over | roads | *gorges
test: «It makes my gorge rise when I smell rotten fish.» What does gorge mean in this sentence? | *Your throat, so the sentence means it makes me feel sick | To climb up a steep hill | To eat a lot of food greedily | A deep valley with a river at the bottom
test: Why are gorges often found with rivers? | Gorges are where fish are made | *Rivers cut through rock over a very long time | Gorges are always flat and wide | Rivers are always built by people
exam: In which sentence does gorge mean to eat greedily? | The bridge crossed the gorge | The walkers looked down into the gorge | Cheddar Gorge is in England | *Bees gorge on honey when they smell smoke
